21.676712328767,"title":"President of Guinea","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Alpha Condé"},{"qid":"Q1006","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q718230","value_label":"Sékouba Konaté","from_t":2009.9205479452055,"to_t":2010.9698630136986,"title":"President of Guinea","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Sékouba Konaté"},{"qid":"Q1006","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q718245","value_label":"Louis Lansana Beavogui","from_t":1984.2322404371585,"to_t":1984.2540983606557,"title":"President of Guinea","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Louis Lansana Beavogui"},{"qid":"Q1006","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q165500","value_label":"Flag of Guinea.svg","from_t":1958.8575342465754,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Guinea","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Guinea"}],"people":{"Q108418519":{"label":"Mamadi Doumbouya","description":"Guinean military officer and politician (1980-)","enwiki":"Mamady Doumbouya","summary":"Mamadi Doumbouya (N'Ko: ߡߊ߬ߡߊߘߌ߫ ߘߎ߲ߓߎߦߊ߫, born 5 December 1984) is a Guinean general serving as the current fifth president of Guinea since 1 October 2021. He maintained his position after he won the 2025 Guinean presidential election in a landslide victory. Doumbouya led a coup d'état on 5 September 2021 that overthrew the previous president, Alpha Condé.\nHe is a member of the Special Forces Group of the Guinean military and a former French legionnaire.","image":"Mamadi Doumbouya 2022.jpg","birth":1984.9262295081967,"death":null},"Q22209348":{"label":"Bah Oury","description":"Guinean politician","enwiki":"Bah Oury","summary":"Amadou Oury Bah (born March 1958) is a Guinean politician who has been the Prime Minister of Guinea since 2024. He has been a member and held leadership positions in the Union of Democratic Forces of Guinea, Union of Democrats for the Rebirth of Guinea, and the Union for Democracy and Development. Prior to his tenure as prime minister he was Minister of National Reconciliation.","image":"Bah Oury.jpg","birth":1958.1616438356164,"death":null},"Q155402":{"label":"Ahmed Sékou Touré","description":"President of Guinea (1922-1984)","enwiki":"Ahmed Sékou Touré","summary":"Ahmed Sékou Touré (var. Sheku Turay or Ture; N'Ko: ߛߋߞߎ߬ ߕߎ߬ߙߋ; 9 January 1922 – 26 March 1984) was a Guinean political leader and African statesman who was the first president of Guinea from 1958 until his death in 1984. Touré was among the primary Guinean nationalists involved in gaining independence of the country from France. He died in the United States in 1984.","image":"Ahmed Sekoú in 1962 at White House cropped.jpg","birth":1922.021917808219,"death":1984.2322404371585},"Q202582":{"label":"Lansana Conté","description":"second President of Guinea (1934–2008)","enwiki":"Lansana Conté","summary":"General Lansana Conté (N'Ko: ߟߊ߲߭ߛߣߊ߬ ߞߐ߲ߕߋ߬; 30 November 1934 – 22 December 2008) was a Guinean politician and military officer who served as the second president of Guinea from 1984 until his death in 2008. Conté came to power in the 1984 Guinean coup d'état.","image":"Lansana Conté at White House 1988.png","birth":1934.9123287671232,"death":2008.9726775956285},"Q314271":{"label":"Moussa Dadis Camara","description":"President of Guinea","enwiki":"Moussa Dadis Camara","summary":"Captain Moussa Dadis Camara (French pronunciation: [musa dadis kamaʁa]; N'Ko: ߡߎߛߊ߫ ߘߊ߬ߘߌߛ ߞߡߊ߬ߙߊ߫ ߫; born 1 January 1964), now called Moïse Dadis Camara ([mɔiz dadis kamaʁa]), is an ex-officer of the Guinean Army who served as the third president of Guinea from 23 December 2008 to 3 December 2009. He was the first chairman of the National Council for Democracy and Development, which seized power in a military coup d'état on 23 December 2008 shortly after the death of long-time president Lansana Conté.\nOn 28 September 2009, protests occurred in the capital Conakry demanding that Camara step down. The security forces responded with force, and several dozen people died.","image":"Dadis Camara portrait.JPG","birth":1964.9918032786886,"death":null},"Q4413":{"label":"Alpha Condé","description":"President of Guinea from 2010 to 2021","enwiki":"Alpha Condé","summary":"Alpha Condé (N'Ko: ߊߟߑߝߊ߫ ߞߐ߲ߘߍ߫; born 4 March 1938) is a Guinean politician who served as the fourth president of Guinea from 2010 to 2021. He spent decades in opposition to a succession of regimes in Guinea, unsuccessfully running against then-President Lansana Conté in the 1993 and 1998 presidential elections and leading the Rally of the Guinean People (RPG), an opposition party.\nStanding again in the 2010 presidential election, Condé was elected president in a second round of voting. Upon his election, he said he would strengthen Guinea as a democracy and fight corruption.","image":"Alpha Conde - World Economic Forum Annual Meeting 2012.jpg","birth":1938.1698630136987,"death":null},"Q718230":{"label":"Sékouba Konaté","description":"President of Guinea","enwiki":"Sékouba Konaté","summary":"General Sékouba Konaté (N'Ko: ߛߋߞߎߓߊ ߞߏ߬ߣߊ߬ߕߋ; born 6 June 1964) is a former general of the Guinean Army who formerly served as the vice president of its military junta, the National Council for Democracy and Development. After attending military academy, he received the nickname \"El Tigre\" for his action in battle, and gained such popularity with the people he was favored to be president of the government. However, he was appointed vice president; but took control of the country when the president was shot in December 2009.","image":null,"birth":1964.4289617486338,"death":null},"Q718245":{"label":"Louis Lansana Beavogui","description":"President Guinea (1923-1984)","enwiki":"Louis Lansana Beavogui","summary":"Louis Lansana Beavogui (N'Ko: ߟߊ߲߭ߛߌ߬ߣߍ߬ ߓߌߦߊߝ߭ߏߜ߭ߌ߫; 28 December 1923 – 19 August 1984) was a Guinean politician. He was Prime Minister from 1972 to 1984 and was briefly acting President in 1984.","image":"Louis Lansana Béavogui (1964).jpg","birth":1923.9890410958903,"death":1984.6311475409836}},"media":{"Flag of Guinea.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Flag_of_Guinea.svg/330px-Flag_of_Guinea.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guinea.svg","author":null,"license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Guinea.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Coat_of_arms_of_Guinea.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Guinea.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Guinea.svg","author":"Turn685","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Mamadi Doumbouya 2022.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Mamadi_Doumbouya_2022.jpg/330px-Mamadi_Doumbouya_2022.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamadi_Doumbouya_2022.jpg","author":"Ambassade des Etats-Unis Conakry, Guinée","license":"Public domain"},"Bah Oury.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Bah_Oury.jpg/330px-Bah_Oury.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bah_Oury.jpg","author":"GdMrjess","license":"CC0"},"Ahmed Sekoú in 1962 at White House cropped.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Ahmed_Seko%C3%BA_in_1962_at_White_House_cropped.jpg/330px-Ahmed_Seko%C3%BA_in_1962_at_White_House_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_Seko%C3%BA_in_1962_at_White_House_cropped.jpg","author":"Robert Knudsen. White House Photographs. John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum, Boston","license":"Public domain"},"Lansana Conté at White House 1988.png":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Lansana_Cont%C3%A9_at_White_House_1988.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lansana_Cont%C3%A9_at_White_House_1988.png","author":"White House Television Office","license":"Public domain"},"Dadis Camara portrait.JPG":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Dadis_Camara_portrait.JPG/330px-Dadis_Camara_portrait.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dadis_Camara_portrait.JPG","author":"Diplomatica","license":"Public domain"},"Alpha Conde - World Economic Forum Annual Meeting 2012.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Alpha_Conde_-_World_Economic_Forum_Annual_Meeting_2012.jpg/330px-Alpha_Conde_-_World_Economic_Forum_Annual_Meeting_2012.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpha_Conde_-_World_Economic_Forum_Annual_Meeting_2012.jpg","author":"World Economic Forum","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"Louis Lansana Béavogui (1964).jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Louis_Lansana_B%C3%A9avogui_%281964%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Lansana_B%C3%A9avogui_(1964).jpg","author":"Renate Patzek","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"}},"names":["GUI","Gine","Guinea","Guinea Conakry","Guinea Francesa","Guinea-Conakri","Guinea-Conakry","Guinee","Guiné","Guiné-Conacri","Guinée","Guinée-Conakry","Gwinea","Repubblica di Guinea","Republic of Guinea","Republiek Guinee","Republique de Guinee","República da Guiné","República de Guinea","République de Guinée","gn","Γουινέα","Δημοκρατία της Γουινέας","Гвинейская Республика","Гвинея","Гвинея-Конакри","Республика Гвинея","جمهورية غينيا","غينيا","گینه","गिनी","ギニア","ギニア・コナクリ","ギニア共和国","ギニー","ギネ","几内亚","几内亚共和国","幾內